.TH MONITOR-DHCP 8 "October 2026" "monitor-dhcp" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
monitor-dhcp \- deterministic DHCPv4 server for a dedicated Ethernet interface
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B monitor-dhcp
.RB [ \-\-config " " PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-check ]
.br
.B monitor-dhcp
.RB [ \-h | \-\-help ]
.br
.B monitor-dhcp
.RB [ \-V | \-\-version ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B monitor-dhcp
serves configured addresses to known MAC addresses and one deterministic
default address to unknown clients on a dedicated Ethernet interface.
It has no address pool, lease database, DNS, routing, or management API.
.PP
The configured server address and subnet mask must already be assigned to the
selected interface.  The interface must be isolated from production networks
and must not have another DHCP server.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Read configuration from
.IR PATH .
The default is
.IR /etc/monitor-dhcp/config.toml .
.TP
.B \-\-check
Validate TOML syntax and static configuration semantics without inspecting the
interface or opening UDP port 67.
.TP
.BR \-h , " " \-\-help
Print command-line help.
.TP
.BR \-V , " " \-\-version
Print the program version.
.SH SYSTEMD SERVICES
The package installs two unit files and does not enable either one
automatically.
.PP
.B monitor-dhcp.service
is the simple, single-interface service.  It reads
.IR /etc/monitor-dhcp/config.toml .
After editing and validating that file, enable the service with:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo -u monitor-dhcp monitor-dhcp --check
sudo systemctl enable --now monitor-dhcp.service
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B monitor-dhcp@.service
is the optional instance template for running one process per interface.  An
instance named
.I monitor-a
reads
.IR /etc/monitor-dhcp/monitor-a.toml .
For example, after creating and validating
.I monitor-a.toml
and
.IR monitor-b.toml ,
start both instances with:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo systemctl enable --now monitor-dhcp@monitor-a.service
sudo systemctl enable --now monitor-dhcp@monitor-b.service
.fi
.RE
.PP
Each configuration must select a different interface.  Do not run the standard
service and an instance, or two instances, against the same interface.
.SH RELOADS
Use
.B systemctl reload
after changing reservations, the default address, subnet mask, or lease time.
An invalid replacement is rejected and the previous in-memory configuration
remains active.  Changes to
.B interface
or
.B server_ip
require a service restart.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/monitor-dhcp/config.toml
Configuration used by the standard service.
.TP
.I /etc/monitor-dhcp/<instance>.toml
Configuration used by
.IR monitor-dhcp@<instance>.service .
.TP
.I /lib/systemd/system/monitor-dhcp.service
Packaged single-interface unit on Debian-family systems.
.TP
.I /lib/systemd/system/monitor-dhcp@.service
Packaged instance template on Debian-family systems.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/monitor-dhcp.service
Packaged single-interface unit on RPM-based systems.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/monitor-dhcp@.service
Packaged instance template on RPM-based systems.
.SH LOGGING
Logs are written to the systemd journal.  Inspect an instance with, for
example:
.PP
.RS
.nf
journalctl -u monitor-dhcp@monitor-a.service
.fi
.RE
.PP
Set
.B RUST_LOG=debug
in a systemd drop-in for transaction-level diagnostics.
.SH SECURITY
The packaged services run as the unprivileged
.B monitor-dhcp
user and receive only
.B CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE
and
.BR CAP_NET_RAW .
Configuration files should be owned by
.B root:monitor-dhcp
with mode 0640.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR journalctl (1),
.BR systemd.unit (5),
.BR systemd.service (5)
.PP
The complete packaged guide is
.IR /usr/share/doc/monitor-dhcp/HOWTO-systemd.md .
